---
import Byline from '../../components/Byline.astro';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Crumbs from '../../components/Crumbs.astro';
import Email from '../../components/Email.astro';
import { SITE } from '../../lib/data';
import { webPageLd } from '../../lib/ld';

const howto = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'HowTo', name: 'How to verify your chainsaw chain size',
  description: 'Three checks, in order of reliability, to confirm the pitch, gauge and drive-link count of the chain your saw takes.',
  totalTime: 'PT5M',
  tool: [{ '@type': 'HowToTool', name: 'Rag and phone flashlight' }, { '@type': 'HowToTool', name: 'Caliper (optional)' }, { '@type': 'HowToTool', name: 'Chalk or marker' }],
  step: [
    { '@type': 'HowToStep', name: 'Read the bar tail stamp', url: SITE.url + '/guides/how-to-verify-chain-size/#method-1', text: 'Switch the saw off, let it cool and wear gloves. Remove the clutch cover and lift the bar and chain off together. Find the stamped text on the flat of the bar near the tail, clean off oil and sawdust, and read bar length, pitch, gauge and the drive-link count.' },
    { '@type': 'HowToStep', name: 'Count the drive links', url: SITE.url + '/guides/how-to-verify-chain-size/#method-2', text: 'Mark one drive link with chalk, then count every drive link around the loop until you reach the mark again.' },
    { '@type': 'HowToStep', name: 'Measure pitch and gauge', url: SITE.url + '/guides/how-to-verify-chain-size/#method-3', text: 'Measure the distance across three consecutive rivets and divide by two for pitch (about 0.650 inch is .325, 0.750 inch is 3/8, 0.808 inch is .404). Measure drive-link thickness with a caliper for gauge: .043, .050, .058 or .063 inch.' },
  ],
};
const desc = "Three checks to confirm your chainsaw chain size: read the bar-tail stamp, count drive links, measure pitch and gauge. Worked examples from real bars.";
const jsonld = [webPageLd("How to Verify Your Chainsaw Chain Size", "/guides/how-to-verify-chain-size/", desc, SITE.dataDate, "WebPage"), howto];
import ShopBlock from '../../components/ShopBlock.astro';
import { guideShop } from '../../lib/shop';
const shop = guideShop('how-to-verify-chain-size');
---
<Base title={"How to Verify Your Chainsaw Chain Size"} description={desc} path="/guides/how-to-verify-chain-size/" nav="verify" crumbs={[{ name: 'Guides', path: '/guides/' }, { name: 'How to verify your chain size', path: '/guides/how-to-verify-chain-size/' }]} jsonld={jsonld} modified={SITE.dataDate}>
  <div class="wrap">
  <div class="pagehead">
    <Crumbs crumbs={[{ name: 'Guides', path: '/guides/' }, { name: 'How to verify your chain size', path: '/guides/how-to-verify-chain-size/' }]} />
    <h1>How to verify your chain size</h1>
    <p class="lede">Three checks, in order of reliability. Do the first one that your bar allows — together they take about five minutes and they settle arguments that model charts (including ours) cannot, because they describe the bar actually fitted to your saw.</p>
    <Byline label="Last updated" />
  </div>

  <section class="block">
    <h2 id="method-1">Method 1 — Read the bar tail stamp</h2>
    <div class="grid2">
      <div>
        <ol class="steps">
          <li>Switch the saw off, let it cool, and wear gloves — a dull chain still cuts skin.</li>
          <li>Remove the clutch cover (usually two nuts on the side of the saw) and lift the bar and chain off together.</li>
          <li>Find the stamped text on the flat of the bar near the tail (the mount end). Clean off oil and sawdust with a rag; a phone flashlight at an angle makes faint stamps readable.</li>
          <li>Read off, in order: bar length, pitch, gauge, and — on most bars — the drive-link count for the correct loop.</li>
        </ol>
        <p><strong>Worked examples from real bars:</strong></p>
        <ul class="plain">
          <li><span class="mono">18″ · .325 · 050 · 72</span> on a Husqvarna 450 Rancher bar → .325″ pitch, .050″ gauge, 72 drive links (Husqvarna H30 / X-CUT SP33G, Oregon 95TXL).</li>
          <li>A Forestry Forum member's bar stamp read <span class="mono">…058-1.5 … 325 72 DL</span> → .325″ pitch, .058″ gauge (1.5&nbsp;mm), 72 drive links. Note the gauge shown both ways — inches and millimetres.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <figure class="diagram" style="margin:0">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 380 250" role="img" aria-label="Close-up diagram of a guide bar tail with the stamp fields labelled">
          <rect x="30" y="80" width="320" height="80" rx="36" class="svg-fill"/>
          <rect x="50" y="110" width="64" height="18" rx="9" class="svg-thin"/>
          <circle cx="152" cy="120" r="8" class="svg-thin"/>
          <text x="196" y="112" class="svg-txt">18″  .325  .050</text>
          <text x="196" y="140" class="svg-txt">72 DL</text>
          <line x1="230" y1="80" x2="230" y2="36" class="svg-acc"/>
          <text x="160" y="24" class="svg-lbl">Stamp sits on the flat,</text>
          <text x="160" y="42" class="svg-lbl">near the tail</text>
          <line x1="84" y1="160" x2="84" y2="204" class="svg-thin"/>
          <text x="40" y="226" class="svg-lbl">Mount slot &amp; oil hole</text>
          <text x="196" y="196" class="svg-lbl">Metric twins: .050″ = 1.3 mm,</text>
          <text x="196" y="214" class="svg-lbl">.058″ = 1.5 mm, .063″ = 1.6 mm</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>Where the stamp lives, and how the same gauge appears in millimetres on many bars.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="rule" />

  <section class="block">
    <h2 id="method-2">Method 2 — Count the drive links</h2>
    <p style="max-width:72ch">If the stamp is worn away, your old chain still knows the answer — as long as it was the right chain. Mark one drive link (the tangs on the underside of the loop) with chalk, then count all the way around and stop when you reach the mark again. Full instructions with a diagram are on the <a href="/guides/chain-size-chart/">identification chart</a>. If the old chain was wrong (it happens — it is the most common reason people land on this site), use Method 3 for pitch and gauge and match the count to your bar length in the model charts.</p>
  </section>

  <hr class="rule" />

  <section class="block">
    <h2 id="method-3">Method 3 — Measure pitch and gauge</h2>
    <div class="grid2">
      <div>
        <h3>Pitch</h3>
        <ol class="steps">
          <li>Measure the distance between the centres of any three consecutive rivets on the chain.</li>
          <li>Divide by two. That is the pitch.</li>
          <li>Results you will actually see: <span class="mono">≈ 0.650″ → .325″</span> pitch (3 rivets span 0.650″), <span class="mono">0.750″ → 3/8″</span>, <span class="mono">0.808″ → .404″</span>. A 3/8″ low-profile chain also spans 0.750″ — it is told apart by its smaller, lower cutters and is what small saws use.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Gauge</h3>
        <ol class="steps">
          <li>Gauge is the thickness of a drive link's tang — the part that rides in the bar groove.</li>
          <li>Measure it with a caliper if you have one: <span class="mono">.043″ / .050″ / .058″ / .063″</span> (1.1 / 1.3 / 1.5 / 1.6&nbsp;mm).</li>
          <li>No caliper? The bar stamp (Method 1) states the gauge the bar was made for, and that is the number that matters — the chain must match the bar groove, not the other way round.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="callout">
      <b>When the stamp and the chart disagree</b>
      <p>Believe the stamp and the count. Bars get swapped, saws get sold with non-standard setups, and manufacturers change factory fitments mid-model-run — shopping results for the Stihl MS 250 note a gauge change on later production. Our model pages show the factory combinations we could verify and flag every disagreement we found instead of quietly picking one.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="rule" />

  <section class="block">
    <h2>Safety, briefly and seriously</h2>
    <p style="max-width:72ch">A chain that is wrong for the bar — wrong gauge, wrong pitch, wrong length forced onto the sprocket — can derail at speed. Never run a chain that does not sit fully in the bar groove, never “make do” with a near-enough loop, and always follow the saw manufacturer's manual for fitting, tensioning and protective equipment. If you are unsure, a dealer will check a bar and chain over the counter in minutes. See our <a href="/terms/">Terms of Use</a> for the full disclaimer.</p>
    <p><a href="/guides/chainsaw-chain-gauge/">Chain gauge</a> · <a href="/guides/3-8-low-profile-vs-standard-chain/">3/8″ low profile vs standard 3/8″</a> · <a href="/guides/oregon-72-vs-73-chain/">Oregon 72 vs 73</a> · <a href="/guides/how-to-install-chainsaw-chain/">Install the chain</a> · <a href="/guides/chainsaw-safety-gear/">Safety gear</a></p>
  </section>

    <ShopBlock groups={shop.groups} title="Tools &amp; parts mentioned (Amazon)" lead="Optional. Verify pitch, gauge and drive links against your bar stamp before buying any chain or bar." />
  </div>
</Base>
